.TH "LMM" "1" "Sep 2026" "lmm 2.0.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-init - Set lmm up: find your games, add them, sign in, import what is there


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm init [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Walk through setting lmm up, once.

.PP
Four steps, each of which you can skip:
.IP "  1." 5
Scan your Steam libraries for moddable games and add the ones you
 pick, filling in their paths and sources from the install itself.
.IP "  2." 5
Set a default game, so you can leave --game off from then on.
.IP "  3." 5
Sign in to each mod source your games use, with that source's own
 instructions.
.IP "  4." 5
Scan the game's mod directory for mods that are already there and
 bring them under management.

.PP
Re-running it is safe and does the sensible thing: a game that is already
configured is marked and not added again, a source that is already
authenticated is not asked for a key, and a default game that is already
set is left as it is. So it doubles as "add the game I just installed".

.PP
This command is interactive. Under --json, or with nothing to read from,
it prints the equivalent commands instead of prompting - a script wants
those, not a wizard.

.PP
Every step is a command of its own if you would rather run them yourself:
\&'lmm game detect', 'lmm game set-default', 'lmm auth login' and
\&'lmm import'.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for init


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


